  "final_text": "All four steps are done and verified against the Kibana 9.5.5 API.\n\n1. **Data view:** \"Kibana Sample Data Flights\" (`d3d7af60-4c81-11e8-b3d7-01146121b73d`, index `kibana_sample_data_flights`) has 30 fields. The main ones are `timestamp` (date), `Carrier` (string), `Cancelled` (boolean), `DestCityName`, `FlightDelayMin`, `AvgTicketPrice`, and the origin/destination geo points.\n\n2. **Dashboard:** \"S4-598f2572 Flights\" (`c7caebfe-c993-4077-94ab-a783e6698a7c`) has exactly two panels, both bound to the data view by reference:\n   - A line chart of flight count over `timestamp` (date histogram).\n   - A bar chart of flight count by `Carrier`, limited to the top 10 carriers. I chose the top-10 limit myself; the request didn't specify one.\n\n3. **Alert rule:** \"S4-598f2572 Cancelled flights\" (`01817c11-494f-40e1-ad70-6fcb1a4b3bc1`) is an Index threshold rule with these settings:\n   - Index `kibana_sample_data_flights`, time field `timestamp`.\n   - Count of documents filtered by `Cancelled : true`, over all documents.\n   - Fires when the count is greater than 10 within a 5-minute window.\n   - Checks every 1 minute.\n   - It has no actions or connectors. The request didn't ask for any, so I left them out.\n   - The rule was created enabled, so no enable call was needed.\n\n4. **Panel rename:** The carrier panel is now titled \"Flights by carrier\". The first panel is unchanged, and the dashboard still has two panels. Reading the rule back shows `enabled: true`. Its schedule is 1m, and its last execution status is `ok`.\n\nThe create response returned a different rule ID than the one in the request path. Kibana assigned `01817c11-\u2026`. A search by name shows exactly one rule with this name, so nothing was duplicated.\n\nTIME_FIELD: timestamp\nRULE_ENABLED: yes",
